<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <groupId>org.sofabuffers</groupId>
  <!-- CORELIB_PLAN §6: the registry name is derived, not a fixed string - the
       organization slug `sofa-buffers` and the word `corelib`, written in the
       registry's own convention. Maven Central has no single package name, so the
       coordinate is groupId org.sofabuffers (the slug as a reverse-DNS group, which
       is what Maven admits) plus artifactId corelib. The normative half is the
       namespace, and every symbol is under org.sofabuffers.sofab. -->
  <artifactId>corelib</artifactId>
  <!-- 0.13.0: source-breaking, in three places. Sofab.API_VERSION is the WIRE
       contract and stays 1 through all of them: the bytes are unchanged.

       (a) IStream.feed now RETURNS the decode outcome —
       DecodeStatus.COMPLETE or INCOMPLETE for the bytes consumed so far — on both
       overloads, and the separate IStream.status() accessor is gone. One fact had
       two readers and they could disagree: 0.12.x carried exactly that drift, a
       refusal thrown from feed while status() still answered COMPLETE for the same
       message. A refusal keeps travelling on the error channel with its code on the
       exception (INVALID_MSG, LIMIT_EXCEEDED, ARGUMENT), so DecodeStatus also loses
       the LIMIT_EXCEEDED constant that existed only for status() to report it: §6.3
       offers a fourth decode outcome OR a terminal failure on the error channel,
       and this port takes the second. Callers that read status() read feed's return
       instead; callers that branched on DecodeStatus.LIMIT_EXCEEDED catch
       SofabException and switch on error().

       (b) A Visitor.arrayBulk destination of a fillable type but shorter than the
       announced count is refused with ARGUMENT, terminally, instead of falling back
       to per-element delivery (§6.6.3; §6.3's table lists "a destination too short
       for the value it was handed" under that code). The fallback was silent data
       loss on a well-formed message: a visitor overriding arrayBulk alone — the
       documented shape for an integer array — had every element delivered to
       inherited no-ops, never saw arrayBulkEnd, and read COMPLETE off the feed that
       dropped them. Declining is untouched: null, or any type the decoder cannot
       fill, is not a destination at all and still falls back per-element.

       (c) The Seq reservations (placeElem, reserveElem, reserveRow*) and
       Seq.checkIndex compare a wrapper array's schema count themselves, and so
       refuse the countless Bound.SCHEMA_BOUNDED with ARGUMENT rather than growing
       the list uncompared. Pass Bound.schema(count) where the schema bounds the
       array: id >= count is INVALID_MSG, a receiver cap stays LIMIT_EXCEEDED, and
       exactly one of the two is ever handed in (§6.2.1). SCHEMA_BOUNDED remains for
       the one case with no second number to compare — a string/blob whose maxlen
       the caller already enforced at the length word, where PayloadAcc has nothing
       to hold it to.
       (0.12.0: source-breaking. The three §6.2.1 receiver caps became arguments on
       the calls generated code already makes — PayloadAcc.string/blob take the cap
       on the announced length, Seq.reserveRow* the cap on the row index — so every
       one of those call sites gained a parameter. The library still holds no limit
       and defines no default for one; Sofab.SCHEMA_BOUNDED is what a caller passes
       where the schema bounds the field instead.
       0.11.0: behaviour-breaking, not source-breaking. OStream.overScratch now
       hands a thread the same OStream instance at every call, re-armed, instead of
       a new one over the shared buffer - so two overScratch streams held at once
       on one thread are now one stream, where before they were two over one
       buffer. Both were already documented as valid only until the same thread
       asks again. Pre-1.0, the minor is where a breaking change lands.
       Sofab.API_VERSION stayed 1 in both. 0.9.0 removed writeSequenceBegin in
       favour of the writeSequenceBeginLazy / writeSequenceEnd /
       writeSequenceEndKeep trio.) -->
  <version>0.13.0</version>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>SofaBuffers CoreLib (Java)</name>
  <description>Dependency-free, allocation-light, streaming Java implementation of the SofaBuffers (Sofab) serialization format.</description>
  <url>https://github.com/sofa-buffers/corelib-java</url>

  <licenses>
    <license>
      <name>MIT License</name>
      <url>https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT</url>
    </license>
  </licenses>

  <properties>
    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    <maven.compiler.release>17</maven.compiler.release>
    <junit.version>6.1.3</junit.version>
    <gson.version>2.14.0</gson.version>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <version>${junit.version}</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!-- Test-only: parse the shared assets/test_vectors.json conformance suite.
         Gson preserves exact precision for unsigned u64 values via getAsBigInteger().
         The runtime library stays dependency-free. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.google.code.gson</groupId>
      <artifactId>gson</artifactId>
      <version>${gson.version}</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <finalName>sofab</finalName>
    <!-- Put the shared conformance vectors on the test classpath straight from
         assets/ (single source of truth, copied verbatim from the documentation repo). -->
    <testResources>
      <testResource>
        <directory>src/test/resources</directory>
      </testResource>
      <testResource>
        <directory>assets</directory>
        <includes>
          <include>test_vectors.json</include>
        </includes>
      </testResource>
    </testResources>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.16.0</version>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.6.0</version>
      </plugin>
      <!-- Code coverage: produces target/site/jacoco/jacoco.xml during `mvn verify`. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
        <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>0.8.15</version>
        <configuration>
          <!-- Benchmark tools are runnable mains, not unit-tested library code. -->
          <excludes>
            <exclude>org/sofabuffers/sofab/bench/**</exclude>
          </excludes>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>prepare-agent</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
          <execution>
            <id>report</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
      <!-- API documentation: `mvn javadoc:javadoc` -> target/reports/apidocs. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.12.0</version>
        <configuration>
          <source>${maven.compiler.release}</source>
          <doctitle>SofaBuffers Java ${project.version}</doctitle>
          <windowtitle>SofaBuffers Java ${project.version}</windowtitle>
          <quiet>true</quiet>
          <notimestamp>true</notimestamp>
          <!-- Benchmark tools are runnable mains, not part of the public API. -->
          <excludePackageNames>org.sofabuffers.sofab.bench</excludePackageNames>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      <!--
        Run the benchmark tools, e.g.:
          mvn -q compile exec:java -Dexec.mainClass=org.sofabuffers.sofab.bench.Perf
          mvn -q compile exec:java -Dexec.mainClass=org.sofabuffers.sofab.bench.Bench
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
        <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.6.4</version>
        <configuration>
          <classpathScope>compile</classpathScope>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
